helpMsg:
Use this only in conjunction with executable code and projects or with quizzes. This macro can only be used in a script tag and gets replaced by the current user input.
To refer to the inputs in a project, use the parameterized macro:

  @input(0)   &lt;== equal to @input
  @input(1)

persistent can be used in two places:

In a section header, persistent: true keeps the section alive when the user navigates to another slide. Scripts, widgets and animations are not re-created when the user comes back. This is also needed if scripts on other slides should be able to send events to this section.

In the main header, persistent: false prevents that anything of this course (states and the entry in the local course index) is stored in the browser.

Example:
  ## Live Clock
  &lt;!--
  persistent: true
  --&gt;

  &lt;script&gt;
  setInterval(() =&gt; send.output(new Date().toLocaleTimeString()), 1000)
  &quot;LIA: wait&quot;
  &lt;/script&gt;

Use macros to deal with repetitive task and to make the document more readable. You can define your own macros within the main and section headers. Start your macro with an (@) sign and give it a name. Separate the name from the body with a colon (:). If you want to parameterize your macro, use @0, @1, ... for the parameter id. It is a simple string substitution mechanism, and the parameters are separated by a comma (,). If you have commas in your parameters, then use back-tics (`) to enclose this parameter. And triple back-tics (```) to enclose multi-line parameters.

Example:

  ## section
  &lt;!--
  @red: &lt;!-- style=&quot;color: red;&quot; --&gt;

  @bold_italic: __@0__ _@1_
  --&gt;

  @red
  This whole paragraph will be read and this will be:
  @bold_italic(I am Bold,`I am Italic,, I am too`).

Use macros to deal with repetitive task and to make the document more readable. Multi-line macros start with an (@your_name) and end with (@end). Parameters can also be passed as to single line macros.

Example:

  ## section
  &lt;!--
  @evalJS
  &lt;script&gt;
    try{
      eval(`@input`);
    } catch (e) {
      var log = e.stack.match(/((.*?):(.*))
.*?(:(d+):(d+))
)/);
      var err_msg = new LiaError(log[1] + &quot; =&gt;  (&quot; + log[4], 1);
      err_msg.add_detail(0, log[3], &quot;error&quot;, log[5]-1, log[6]);
      throw err_msg;
    }
  &lt;/script&gt;
  @end
  --&gt;

  eval this code with erros:

  ```js
  let x = 12;
  x * c;
  ```
  @evalJS

If you have a more complex multi-line macro definition, you can also use the following style so that it gets rendered at least in a readable manner by Github for example. Thus, you can place your macro call into into the code-block header, the first (optional) language parameter gets ignored and, whereby the following elements within the block are passed as the last multi-line parameter to that call.

Example:

    ## Macro
    &lt;!--
    link:   https://pannellum.org/css/style.css
            https://cdn.pannellum.org/2.4/pannellum.css

    script: https://cdn.pannellum.org/2.4/pannellum.js

    @panorama
    &lt;div id=&quot;panorama_@0&quot; style=&quot;width: 100%; height: 400px;&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;script&gt;
    pannellum.viewer(&#x27;panorama_@0&#x27;, {
        &quot;type&quot;: &quot;equirectangular&quot;,
        &quot;panorama&quot;: &quot;@1&quot;,
        &quot;autoLoad&quot;: false,
        &quot;hotSpots&quot;: [@2]
    });
    &lt;/script&gt;
    @end
    --&gt;

    ```json @panorama(&quot;0&quot;,https://pannellum.org/images/cerro-toco-0.jpg)
    {
        &quot;pitch&quot;: 14.1,
        &quot;yaw&quot;: 1.5,
        &quot;type&quot;: &quot;info&quot;,
        &quot;text&quot;: &quot;Baltimore Museum of Art&quot;,
        &quot;URL&quot;: &quot;https://artbma.org/&quot;
    },
    {
        &quot;pitch&quot;: -0.9,
        &quot;yaw&quot;: 144.4,
        &quot;type&quot;: &quot;info&quot;,
        &quot;text&quot;: &quot;North Charles Street&quot;
    }
    ```

Header block: JavaScript that is executed once when the course is loaded, e.g. to configure a library or to define global helper functions. It is also imported by other courses that import this one.

Example:
  &lt;!--
  script: https://cdn.example.com/library.js

  @onload
  window.greet = (name) =&gt; &quot;Hello &quot; + name
  @end
  --&gt;

  &lt;script&gt;greet(&quot;World&quot;)&lt;/script&gt;

Header block: CSS rules for the whole course. Combine it with class attributes to style single elements.

Example:
  &lt;!--
  @style
  .highlight {
    color: crimson;
    font-weight: bold;
  }
  @end
  --&gt;

  This is **important**&lt;!-- class=&quot;highlight&quot; --&gt;.

Calls a macro with parameters. Inside the definition, @0, @1, ... are replaced by the parameters in order. The last parameter can also be passed as a code block directly above the call (see lia-macro-call-block).

Commas inside a parameter can be protected with backticks, but this is not reliable in every LiaScript version. If possible, avoid commas in parameters.

Example:
  &lt;!--
  @highlight: &lt;b style=&quot;color: @0&quot;&gt;@1&lt;/b&gt;
  --&gt;

  @highlight(red, simple text)

The details and summary tags are standard HTML tags and GitHub also supports their usage with Markdown. These tags offer a neat way to define something what is nowadays called accordion. Thus, your user can click on the summary text to make the body of the details-tag appear.

Example:
  &lt;details&gt;

  &lt;summary&gt;**Honest Textbook ads (click to enlarge)**&lt;/summary&gt;

  !?[If High School and College Textbooks Were Honest - Honest Ads](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lhSjYT7pWkw)

  &lt;/details&gt;

Everything inside &lt;lia-keep&gt; is passed through as raw HTML: no Markdown parsing and no indentation checks. Useful for complex hand-written HTML, such as tables with rowspan or colspan.

Example:
  &lt;lia-keep&gt;
  &lt;table&gt;
    &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td rowspan=&quot;2&quot;&gt;A&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;B&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;
    &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;C&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;
  &lt;/table&gt;
  &lt;/lia-keep&gt;

A slider that re-runs the script on every change. @input is replaced by the current number.

Example:
  &lt;script input=&quot;range&quot; value=&quot;0&quot; min=&quot;0&quot; max=&quot;360&quot; step=&quot;1&quot;&gt;
  let deg = @input
  &quot;sin(&quot; + deg + &quot;°) = &quot; + Math.sin(deg * Math.PI / 180).toFixed(3)
  &lt;/script&gt;

A dropdown (select) or a group of radio buttons. The options are separated by | in the options attribute, value sets the initial selection.

Example:
  &lt;script input=&quot;radio&quot; value=&quot;1&quot; options=&quot;1|2|3|Hello World&quot;&gt;
  &quot;Selected option: @input&quot;
  &lt;/script&gt;

A button that runs the script only when it is clicked. default sets the text that is shown before the first click. Use input=&quot;button&quot; instead to run it also once on load.

Example:
  &lt;script input=&quot;submit&quot; default=&quot;roll the dice&quot;&gt;
  Math.floor(Math.random() * 6) + 1
  &lt;/script&gt;

Connect scripts: a script with output=&quot;name&quot; publishes its current result. Other scripts on the same slide can read it with @input(`name`) (with backticks) and re-run automatically when it changes.

If two scripts depend on each other, give both a default=&quot;...&quot; value. Otherwise the first calculation fails.

Example:
  &lt;script input=&quot;checkbox&quot; value=&quot;true&quot; output=&quot;P&quot; default=&quot;true&quot;&gt;
  @input
  &lt;/script&gt;
  AND
  &lt;script input=&quot;checkbox&quot; value=&quot;false&quot; output=&quot;Q&quot; default=&quot;false&quot;&gt;
  @input
  &lt;/script&gt;
  =
  &lt;script&gt;
  @input(`P`) &amp;&amp; @input(`Q`)
  &lt;/script&gt;

Formats the result of a script with the JavaScript Intl API for the given locale. Only the display changes, connected scripts still get the raw value.

  format=&quot;number&quot;        1234.5 -&gt; 1.234,5 (de-DE)
  format=&quot;datetime&quot;      dates and times
  format=&quot;relativetime&quot;  &quot;in 3 days&quot;
  format=&quot;list&quot;          &quot;a, b and c&quot;
  format=&quot;pluralrules&quot;   plural category

Example:
  &lt;script input=&quot;date&quot; value=&quot;2024-05-16&quot; format=&quot;datetime&quot; locale=&quot;de&quot;&gt;
  &quot;@input&quot;
  &lt;/script&gt;

A self-updating script component: return &quot;LIA: wait&quot; (or call send.wait()) to keep it running, then call send.output(...) for every update. The process keeps running in the background when the user switches slides, until send.stop() is called.

send.output only works in standalone scripts, not in scripts of code blocks (use send.lia there).

Shortcuts: send.wait(), send.stop(), send.clear()

Example:
  &lt;script&gt;
  setInterval(() =&gt; send.output(new Date().toLocaleTimeString()), 1000)
  &quot;LIA: wait&quot;
  &lt;/script&gt;

Every script runs in its own local scope, so variables of one script are not visible in another. To share values between scripts, attach them to window explicitly. For live updates between scripts, prefer output=&quot;...&quot; and @input(`...`) (see lia-script-connect).

Example:
  &lt;script&gt; window.a = 22 ** 3 &lt;/script&gt;
  &lt;script&gt; window.a / 2 &lt;/script&gt;

LIA.classroom lets scripts exchange messages with all participants of a live classroom session (peer-to-peer). It is only active while a classroom is connected.

  LIA.classroom.connected                 true if a session is active
  LIA.classroom.on(&quot;connect&quot;, cb)         also &quot;disconnect&quot;
  LIA.classroom.publish(&quot;topic&quot;, data)    send to all peers
  LIA.classroom.subscribe(&quot;topic&quot;, cb)    returns an id
  LIA.classroom.unsubscribe(id)

Example:
  &lt;script&gt;
  LIA.classroom.on(&quot;connect&quot;, () =&gt; send.output(&quot;connected&quot;))
  &quot;LIA: wait&quot;
  &lt;/script&gt;

A script directly after a survey can check or process the input before it is accepted. Empty input is rejected by default. The last expression decides:

  true                              -&gt; accept the input
  false                             -&gt; reject it silently
  send.lia(&quot;message&quot;, [], false)    -&gt; reject it with a message

Wrap @input in backticks for text surveys. For choice surveys @input is the selected value(s). console.log does not show anything here, use alert(...) while developing.

Example:
      [[___]]
      &lt;script&gt;
        let input = `@input`.trim();
        if (input.length &gt; 4) true
        else send.lia(&quot;Please provide some meaningful input&quot;, [], false)
      &lt;/script&gt;

replace:
[[___]]
<script>
  let input = `@input`.trim();

  ${1:if (input.length > 4) {
    true
  \} else if (input.length == 0) {
    send.lia("Please enter some text", [], false)
  \} else {
    send.lia("Please provide some meaningful input", [], false)
  \}}
</script>

helpMsg:
SVG can be written directly into the document. It must not be put into a code block, otherwise it is shown as code. Use viewBox to make the image scale with the slide width.

Dark mode inverts the colors of LiaScript but not the hard-coded colors of an SVG, so set a background and explicit colors on the svg element.

Example:
  &lt;svg viewBox=&quot;0 0 200 100&quot; style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;
    &lt;circle cx=&quot;50&quot; cy=&quot;50&quot; r=&quot;40&quot; fill=&quot;lightblue&quot; stroke=&quot;blue&quot;/&gt;
    &lt;text x=&quot;50&quot; y=&quot;55&quot; font-size=&quot;10&quot; text-anchor=&quot;middle&quot;&gt;Circle&lt;/text&gt;
  &lt;/svg&gt;

foreignObject places LiaScript content (Markdown, formulas, quizzes, animations, ...) at a position inside an SVG. x, y, width and height are required and use the coordinates of the viewBox.

Example:
  &lt;svg viewBox=&quot;0 0 280 200&quot; style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;
  &lt;foreignObject x=&quot;130&quot; y=&quot;0&quot; width=&quot;200&quot; height=&quot;80&quot;&gt;
  Circumference:

  $$C = 2 \pi r$$
  &lt;/foreignObject&gt;
  &lt;circle cx=&quot;100&quot; cy=&quot;100&quot; r=&quot;80&quot; fill=&quot;lightblue&quot; stroke=&quot;blue&quot;/&gt;
  &lt;line x1=&quot;100&quot; y1=&quot;100&quot; x2=&quot;180&quot; y2=&quot;100&quot; stroke=&quot;red&quot;/&gt;
  &lt;foreignObject x=&quot;110&quot; y=&quot;78&quot; width=&quot;100&quot; height=&quot;30&quot;&gt; Radius: $r$ &lt;/foreignObject&gt;
  &lt;/svg&gt;

