For diagrams that cannot be generated from a table, use the lia-chart element directly. The option attribute takes a complete Apache ECharts configuration. Scripts can also generate it dynamically: return &quot;HTML: &lt;lia-chart option=&#x27;...&#x27;&gt;&lt;/lia-chart&gt;&quot;.

Example:
  &lt;lia-chart option=&quot;{
    title: { text: &#x27;Squares&#x27; },
    xAxis: { type: &#x27;value&#x27; },
    yAxis: { type: &#x27;value&#x27; },
    series: [{ type: &#x27;line&#x27;, data: [[0,0],[1,1],[2,4],[3,9]] }]
  }&quot;&gt;&lt;/lia-chart&gt;

A script directly after a task list runs every time a box is checked or unchecked, but its result is not shown. Give it an output=&quot;name&quot;, then other scripts on the same slide can read the states with @input(`name`) and react to them. The value is an array of booleans, one per task, e.g. [true, false].

Example:
  - [X] Biology
  - [ ] Chemistry
  &lt;script output=&quot;tasks&quot;&gt;&quot;@input&quot;&lt;/script&gt;

  &lt;script style=&quot;width: 100%&quot;&gt;
  try {
    let task = @input(`tasks`)

    if (task[0]) {
      send.liascript(`## Biology

  Hey, great, you want to learn something about Biology.

  * resource 1
  * resource 2`)
    } else send.clear()
  } catch(e) { }
  &lt;/script&gt;

replace:
- [${1: }] ${2:task-definition}
- [${3: }] ${4:task-definition}
<script output="${5:tasks}">"@input"</script>

<script>
  let tasks = @input(`${5:tasks}`)
  ${6:tasks.every(done => done) ? "All done!" : "Keep going ..."}
</script>

helpMsg:
Text quiz with a custom check. The script is placed directly after the quiz. Before it runs, @input is replaced by the raw text the user typed, so wrap it in backticks (`@input`) to get a JavaScript string. The last expression of the script has to be true (solved) or false (not solved).

Example:
  What is the color of the sky?

      [[blue]]
      &lt;script&gt;
        let input = `@input`.trim().toLowerCase();
        input === &quot;blue&quot; || input === &quot;azure&quot;;
      &lt;/script&gt;

replace:
[[${1:solution}]]
<script>
  ${2:// @input is replaced by the raw user input, so wrap it in backticks
  let input = `@input`.trim().toLowerCase();
  input === "solution" || input === "answer";}
</script>

helpMsg:
A complete quiz template that combines everything: a question, the quiz, hints, a script that checks the input and a solution block. Remember: no blank line before the opening asterisks of the solution.

Example:
  What is $37 + 15$?

      [[52]]
      [[?]] The solution is larger than 50.
      [[?]] It is an even number.
      &lt;script&gt;
        let input = `@input`.trim();
        input === &quot;52&quot;
      &lt;/script&gt;
      ****************************************

      $37 + 15 = 52$

      ****************************************

replace:
[(${1: })] ${2:This is wrong.}
[(${3:X})] ${4:The correct option.}
[(${5: })] ${6:Still not right.}

[[${1: }]] ${2:Add as many options as you want.}
[[${3:X}]] ${4:The X marks the correct answer!}
[[${5: }]] ${6:... this is wrong ...}
[[${7:X}]] ${8:... this has to be checked too ...}
